mmittee":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-07","committee":"Cabinet","time":"9.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-13","committee":"Corporate Governance & Audit Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-07-14","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-01","committee":"Cabinet","time":"9.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"CCLA Cautious Multi-Asset Fund suffered \u00a3312k capital loss with officers requiring close monitoring of the investment","lead":"Capital losses in a conservative pooled fund combined with the need for active management intervention suggests fund selection risk or broader market deterioration affecting supposedly low-risk investments.","iso":"2026-09-01","paper_title":"16.0 Treasury Management Outturn Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33065/33065.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Commercial property portfolio vacancy rates have doubled to 5.4%, with explicit council warning that capital values could fall below original purchase price","lead":"The \u00a313.13m commercial property investment represents a concentrated asset class showing material deterioration; doubling vacancy rates and explicit capital depreciation warnings indicate income streams are less reliable than historical performance and\u2026","iso":"2026-09-01","paper_title":"16.0 Treasury Management Outturn Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33065/33065.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Bank account overdraft of \u00a34m in April 2026 due to officer error, revealing cash management control weaknesses","lead":"Lenders assess operational risk and control environment; a \u00a34m overdraft caused by human error signals that cash management processes may be vulnerable to failure, even in a council with large liquid reserves.","iso":"2026-09-01","paper_title":"16.0 Treasury Management Outturn Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33065/33065.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"16.0 Treasury Management Outturn Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33065/33065.pdf","headline":"CCLA Cautious Multi-Asset Fund suffered \u00a3312k capital loss with officers requiring close monitoring of the investment","detail":"The council's pooled fund portfolio generated an average capital gain of only 1.16% (below the sector average of 1.31%), depressed by a \u00a3312k capital loss in the CCLA Cautious Multi-Asset Fund. Officers report they are now liaising with both the fund manager and the council's treasury advisor to track this fund closely.","category":"other","matters":"Capital losses in a conservative pooled fund combined with the need for active management intervention suggests fund selection risk or broader market deterioration affecting supposedly low-risk investments.","figures":"\u00a3312k capital loss from CCLA Cautious Multi-Asset Fund; portfolio capital gain 1.16% vs sector average 1.31%","quote":"This is primarily due to the underperformance of capital loss of \u00a3312k from CCLA Cautious Multi-Asset Fund. The Officers are liaising with the Fund Manager and the Council's Treasury Advisor Arlingclose to track closely on the movement of this fund.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"16.0 Treasury Management Outturn Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33065/33065.pdf","headline":"Commercial property portfolio vacancy rates have doubled to 5.4%, with explicit council warning that capital values could fall below original purchase price","detail":"Investment property vacancies increased sharply from 2.27% (2024-25) to 5.4% (2025-26), with rental income falling from \u00a30.86m to \u00a30.77m. The council explicitly warns that commercial property values face downward pressure from market uncertainty, interest rates, and energy costs, and that capital values \"could potentially fall below the original purchase price.\"","category":"income","matters":"The \u00a313.13m commercial property investment represents a concentrated asset class showing material deterioration; doubling vacancy rates and explicit capital depreciation warnings indicate income streams are less reliable than historical performance and asset values are at risk.","figures":"Vacancy rate 5.4% (from 2.27%); rental income \u00a30.77m (from \u00a30.86m); portfolio value \u00a313.13m","quote":"Global events continue to contribute to a somewhat cautious commercial property market, compounded by uncertainty around interest rates and significant energy price rises. Downward pressure on rents and risk of tenant default is expected to continue locally, with investment yields reflecting the added risk to future income streams which result in lower capital values. These values could potentially fall below the original purchase price of the investment properties","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-01","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"16.0 Treasury Management Outturn Report 2025-26","paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33065/33065.pdf","headline":"Bank account overdraft of \u00a34m in April 2026 due to officer error, revealing cash management control weaknesses","detail":"On 30 April 2026, the council's bank account fell into overdraft by \u00a34m due to an officer's error, incurring a \u00a31,689 bank charge. The overdraft was resolved the next day and the council reports enhanced controls have been implemented, but the incident demonstrates that despite strong reserves and treasury oversight, daily cash management procedures can fail.","category":"governance","matters":"Lenders assess operational risk and control environment; a \u00a34m overdraft caused by human error signals that cash management processes may be vulnerable to failure, even in a council with large liquid reserves.","figures":"\u00a34m overdraft, \u00a31,689 bank charge incurred","quote":"On the day of 30 April 2026, the bank account was overdrawn by \u00a34m due to an officer's error, resulting in a bank charge of \u00a31,689. The overdraft was resolved the next day.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-13","committee":"Corporate Governance & Audit Committee","paper_title":"26 CDC - Audit Planning Report","paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s32810/32810.pdf","headline":"The council's net pension liability is \u00a31.19m, a material balance requiring specialist actuarial review and IAS 19 compliance work.","detail":"The council is a member of the West Sussex Pension Fund. The net liability totalled \u00a31.19m at 31 March 2025. Accounting for this involves significant estimation and judgement. Auditors will engage their own actuarial specialists to assess the actuary's work, review assumptions, and verify the asset ceiling calculation under IFRIC 14.","category":"other","matters":"Pension liabilities are sensitive to actuarial assumptions (discount rates, inflation, longevity). Errors in valuation or disclosure could materially misstate the balance sheet. Lenders monitor pension deficits as a measure of the council's long-term financial obligations.","figures":"Net pension liability: \u00a31.19m at 31 March 2025","quote":"The Council's pension fund deficit is a material estimated balance and the Code requires that this liability be disclosed on the Council's balance sheet... Accounting for this scheme involves significant estimation and judgement.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-13","committee":"Corporate Governance & Audit Committee","paper_title":"26 CDC - Audit Planning Report","paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s32810/32810.pdf","headline":"The council is implementing new property valuation rules (CIPFA Bulletin 22) for the first time in 2025/26, affecting \u00a3162m of land and buildings assets.","detail":"The council holds \u00a3162m in property, plant and equipment (including \u00a3145m in land and buildings). For 2025/26, the council must apply new CIPFA Bulletin 22 requirements mandating quinquennial revaluation or five-year rolling programmes with annual indexation. Auditors have identified this as a key audit focus area requiring challenge of valuation assumptions and indices.","category":"capital","matters":"A significant asset base undergoing new accounting treatment creates misstatement risk. Errors in applying new valuation guidance, selecting indices, or determining revaluation triggers could materially overstate or understate asset values and inflate or deflate financial position.","figures":"PPE: \u00a3162m; Land and buildings: \u00a3145m; Additions in 2024/25: \u00a32.76m","quote":"In the 2025/26 financial statements the Council will be required to apply revised provisions in the CIPFA Code of Practice on Local Authority Accounting and associated guidance in CIPFA Bulletin 22 which reassesses the current regime of valuation for non-investment assets across the public sector. The guidance mandates a quinquennial revaluation or a five-year rolling programme for formal valuations, supported by annual indexation in the intervening years.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-01"},"name":"Chichester","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-20","since_iso":"2026-08-21","distress":false,"headline":"CCLA Cautious Multi-Asset Fund suffered \u00a3312k capital loss with officers requiring close monitoring of the investment","lead":"Capital losses in a conservative pooled fund combined with the need for active management intervention suggests fund selection risk or broader market deterioration affecting supposedly low-risk investments.","also":["2 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Overview & Scrutiny Committee on 2026-09-29","n_findings":3,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"16.0 Treasury Management Outturn Report 2025-26","top_paper_url":"https://chichester.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s33065/33065.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":49,"news_drafts_total":3}}